# ------------------------------------------------------------------ IPsec VPNs
qs = [
 Q('What is the difference between a site-to-site and a remote access VPN?', 'Site-to-site connects whole networks through gateways and is invisible to hosts; remote access connects individual users running VPN client software',
   ['Site-to-site needs client software on every PC', 'Remote access only works inside the LAN', 'They are the same'],
   'Site-to-site: router or firewall at each office. Remote access: Cisco Secure Client (AnyConnect) or similar on a laptop, connecting to a headend.'),
 Q('Which IPsec protocol provides encryption?', 'ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload)', ['AH (Authentication Header)', 'IKE', 'GRE'],
   'ESP (IP protocol 50) gives confidentiality, integrity and authentication. AH (protocol 51) gives integrity and authentication but no encryption, and it doesn\'t work through NAT.'),
 Q('Why doesn\'t AH work through NAT?', 'AH\'s integrity check covers the IP header, so NAT changing the address makes the check fail',
   ['AH uses UDP 500', 'AH encrypts the IP header', 'NAT blocks all IPsec'],
   'ESP doesn\'t protect the outer IP header, and NAT Traversal wraps ESP in UDP 4500 so it passes NAT.'),
 Q('Which protocol and port does IKE use to negotiate IPsec tunnels?', 'UDP 500 (UDP 4500 with NAT Traversal)', ['TCP 443', 'IP protocol 50', 'TCP 22'],
   'IKE (Internet Key Exchange) agrees encryption and keys. IKEv2 is the modern version.'),
 Q('What is the difference between IPsec tunnel mode and transport mode?', 'Tunnel mode encrypts the whole original packet and adds a new IP header; transport mode protects only the payload and keeps the original IP header',
   ['Tunnel mode uses TCP; transport mode uses UDP', 'Transport mode encrypts more', 'Tunnel mode is only for remote access'],
   'Site-to-site VPNs use tunnel mode so the inside addresses are hidden. Transport mode is for host-to-host, or protecting a GRE tunnel.'),
 Q('Which mode do site-to-site VPN gateways normally use?', 'Tunnel mode', ['Transport mode', 'Promiscuous mode', 'Monitor mode'], 'The gateways add their own public IP header around the private packet.'),
 Q('Which three does IPsec provide?', ['Confidentiality (encryption)', 'Integrity', 'Origin authentication'], ['Bandwidth guarantees', 'Address assignment', 'Routing'],
   'Plus anti-replay protection. Algorithms: AES for encryption, SHA for integrity, Diffie-Hellman for key exchange, pre-shared keys or certificates for authentication.', card='Name the security services IPsec provides.'),
 Q('Why is GRE often combined with IPsec?', 'GRE can carry multicast and routing protocol traffic such as OSPF; IPsec then encrypts the GRE tunnel',
   ['GRE adds encryption to IPsec', 'IPsec needs GRE to pass NAT', 'GRE replaces IKE'],
   'Plain IPsec only carries unicast IP. GRE over IPsec gives you a routable, encrypted tunnel.'),
 Q('Which type of remote access VPN needs only a web browser or a lightweight client and runs over TCP 443?', 'SSL/TLS VPN', ['IPsec site-to-site', 'GRE', 'L2TP without IPsec'],
   'TLS VPNs pass through almost any firewall because they look like HTTPS. Cisco Secure Client supports both TLS and IPsec IKEv2.'),
 Q('What does Diffie-Hellman do in IPsec?', 'Lets the two peers create a shared secret key over an untrusted network without ever sending the key', ['Encrypts the data', 'Hashes packets for integrity', 'Assigns IP addresses to clients'],
   'Higher DH groups are stronger.'),
]
